  "final_text": "I finished all four steps. Reading the dashboard and the rule back after the edit shows the changes were saved.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) points at the index `kibana_sample_data_flights`. Its time field is `timestamp`. Its fields are:\n   - **Numbers:** `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`, `DistanceMiles`, `FlightDelayMin`, `FlightTimeMin`, `dayOfWeek`, `hour_of_day`\n   - **True/false:** `Cancelled`, `FlightDelay`\n   - **Locations:** `OriginLocation`, `DestLocation`\n   - **Text:** `Carrier`, `FlightNum`, `FlightTimeHour`, `FlightDelayType`, and the origin and destination fields (airport, airport ID, city, country, region, weather)\n   - Plus Elasticsearch's built-in fields such as `_id` and `_index`\n\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-ccef6d74 Flights\" (id `b0f78761-550a-4242-b0a5-4146c7c6d26e`) has exactly two panels, both on that data view:\n   - a line chart of the number of flights over time;\n   - a bar chart of the number of flights for each carrier.\n\n   I set the dashboard's time range to the last 7 days, which you didn't specify.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-ccef6d74 Cancelled flights\" (id `73a0ed32-d693-475f-a9fc-39b7f5322c1a`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. Every minute it counts flights with `Cancelled: true` in the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it. It has no actions, so it fires alerts but doesn't send notifications.\n\n4. **Edit and check:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\", and the dashboard still has two panels. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, and its first run completed with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
